Finite-Rate Catalysis-Induced Phase Separation: 

A Classification and Regime Map

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

v0.4

Abstract

The two existing theories of catalysis-induced phase separation (CIPS) — the phenomenological Flory–Huggins theory of Cotton, Golestanian, and Agudo-Canalejo (2022) and the microscopic particle-based theory of Jardat et al. (arXiv:2611.12735, 2026) — both derive their instability criteria under the assumption that chemical reactions are fast relative to diffusion. This assumption is constitutive, not optional: it permits a local equilibrium approximation (LEA) that eliminates the reactive degrees of freedom and reduces the ternary enzyme–substrate–product mixture to an effective nonreciprocal binary mixture. We ask whether CIPS survives when this limit is relaxed, and whether the instability changes class within the Cross–Hohenberg taxonomy. Three facts frame the question. First, the LEA is the structural step through which the instability criterion of Jardat et al. is derived; the finite-rate question is therefore well posed as the regime where this step fails. Second, Raßhofer and Frey (arXiv:2603.17690, 2026) have established finite-turnover coarsening arrest in a related solute-mixture model; their result is the benchmark, not a competing novelty claim. Third, mass conservation in a system with two conservation laws does not forbid a finite-wavelength (Type Iₛ) instability: conserved-Turing bands are known in nonreciprocal conserved systems, and the conserved-Hopf (Type IIₒ) branch must be tested alongside the stationary one. We report the exact 3×3 linear stability analysis, which (i) reproduces the LEA criterion of Eq. (1) term by term as δ_q → 0, (ii) yields the σ₂ and σ₄ coefficients in closed form, and (iii) identifies the sign pattern (σ₂ < 0, σ₄ > 0, σ₆ < 0) as the analytic criterion for a Type Iₛ band at finite Γ. We propose a three-phase programme: exact symbolic linear stability, continuum and Brownian-dynamics parameter sweeps prioritised in the δ_q ≈ 1 resonance region, and a pre-registered coarsening test. The project has two robust outcomes: either a genuine finite-wavelength instability in the Jardat mechanism, or a precise regime map showing where Eq. (1) applies, how it fails at finite Γ, and where finite chemistry matters biologically.

---

1. State of the art and the gap

1.1 The LEA as a constitutive premise

Cotton, Golestanian, and Agudo-Canalejo derive their CIPS instability criterion "in the limit of fast reaction rates," using a mapping to an effective free energy. Jardat et al. inherit the same assumption: their abstract states explicitly that "upon eliminating the fast chemical degrees of freedom, the reactive ternary mixture maps onto an effective binary mixture [with] nonreciprocal interactions." The LEA is not an external simplification appended to an otherwise general derivation; it is the step through which the instability criterion is obtained. The effective diffusion matrix

\mathcal{D}_{\rm LEA} = \begin{pmatrix} D_e(1+\rho_e\mathcal{B}_{ee}) & D_e\rho_e\mathcal{B}_{es} \\ D_s^{\rm eff}\rho_{sp}\mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff} & D_s^{\rm eff} \end{pmatrix}
\tag{1}

with $\mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff} = \mathcal{B}_{es} + \partial_{\rho_e}\ln D_s^{\rm eff}$ and $\det\mathcal{D}_{\rm LEA} < 0$ as the instability condition, is the entire content of the LEA reduction. The finite-rate question is therefore precisely: what happens to the classification of the dispersion relation when the elimination that produces Eq. (1) is no longer valid?

1.2 Closest prior work: Raßhofer–Frey as benchmark, not competitor

Raßhofer and Frey (arXiv:2603.17690, March 2026) study an incompressible mixture of thermodynamically identical solutes with unequal diffusivities and driven interconversion. Their abstract states: "In the limit of fast chemical turnover, the system always coarsens… Beyond this limit, we develop a sharp-interface theory that predicts the onset of arrested coarsening, stationary droplet sizes, and nucleation conditions." They have therefore already established that finite turnover arrests coarsening in a related model. The mechanism there is chemical enrichment of the fast-diffusing species in the dense phase, which suppresses Ostwald ripening. This is the benchmark against which any coarsening result in the Jardat mechanism must be compared.

The Jardat mechanism is structurally distinct in its feedback topology: the phase-separating field is the enzyme density, coupled to solutes through excluded volume only (no attraction), and the reaction rate depends on the local enzyme density. In Raßhofer–Frey, a prescribed thermodynamic interaction drives separation and chemistry modulates it. In Jardat, there is no thermodynamic attraction at all; the separation is a purely nonequilibrium effect from diffusivity contrast and nonreciprocal coupling. This distinction is the narrow, precise basis of the present work's novelty.

1.3 Novelty statement (narrowed)

To our knowledge, as of 10 November 2026, no study has computed the finite-rate linear stability classification, threshold correction, and coarsening behaviour of the enzyme-mediated, excluded-volume CIPS mechanism of Jardat et al. The finite-turnover arrest phenomenon has been established by Raßhofer and Frey; what is open is its instability class in this specific mechanism, where no attractive interaction exists and the phase field is the enzyme itself. The novelty is therefore not "finite-rate arrest in CIPS" but "finite-rate classification of the excluded-volume CIPS mechanism."

1.4 Prior art that must be cited

The type-I hypothesis is not quixotic: finite-wavelength (conserved-Turing) instabilities are known to be generic in two-field conserved systems with nonreciprocal coupling. The relevant literature includes Frohoff-Hülsmann & Thiele (small-scale Turing instability in nonreciprocal Cahn–Hilliard), Frohoff-Hülsmann, Thiele & Pismen (resonance, oscillatory behaviour, and coarsening suppression in nonreciprocal Cahn–Hilliard), and Greve et al. (2024) (amplitude equations for the conserved-Hopf bifurcation). These works provide both the existence proof for Type Iₛ in this model class and the weakly nonlinear framework needed to interpret any oscillatory instability found in Phase B.

