2. Theoretical framework

2.1 Full ternary model without LEA

We retain the dilute ternary model of Jardat et al.: enzyme ($e$), substrate ($s$), and product ($p$) obey overdamped Langevin dynamics with bare diffusion coefficients $D_i$, equal enzyme–solute excluded volumes $\sigma_{es} = \sigma_{ep}$, and vanishing solute–solute interactions. The reaction fluxes are

R_e = 0, \qquad R_{s,p} = \pm\{k_{\rm spo}[\rho_p - e^{\varepsilon}\rho_s] + k_{\rm cat}\rho_e[\rho_p e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)} - \rho_s]\}.
\tag{2}

The full reaction–diffusion equations are $\partial_t\rho_i = \nabla\cdot\sum_j \mathcal{D}_{ij}\nabla\rho_j + R_i$, with the 3×3 diffusion matrix

\mathcal{D} = \begin{pmatrix} D_e(1+\rho_e\mathcal{B}_{ee}) & D_e\rho_e\mathcal{B}_{es} & D_e\rho_e\mathcal{B}_{ep} \\ D_s\rho_s\mathcal{B}_{es} & D_s & 0 \\ D_p\rho_p\mathcal{B}_{ep} & 0 & D_p \end{pmatrix},
\tag{3}

and $\mathcal{B}_{ij} = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi\sigma_{ij}^3$.

2.2 Exact linear stability without LEA

Linearizing about $(\rho_e^0, \rho_s^0, \rho_p^0)$ and Fourier-transforming in space, perturbations obey

[\sigma(q)\mathbb{I} + q^2\mathcal{D} - \mathcal{J}]\,\delta\boldsymbol{\rho}(q) = 0,
\tag{4}

with $\mathcal{J} = \partial R/\partial\boldsymbol{\rho}$ evaluated at the homogeneous state. Non-trivial solutions require $\det[\sigma\mathbb{I} + q^2\mathcal{D} - \mathcal{J}] = 0$, a cubic in $\sigma$:

\sigma^3 + a_2(q^2)\sigma^2 + a_1(q^2)\sigma + a_0(q^2) = 0.
\tag{5}

2.3 Reaction Jacobian and the reactive eigenvalue

At the homogeneous state the Jacobian takes the conserved-row form

\mathcal{J} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ J_{se} & -\Gamma_s & -\Gamma_p \\ -J_{se} & \Gamma_s & \Gamma_p \end{pmatrix},
\tag{6}

with

\Gamma_s = -\left[k_{\rm spo}e^{\varepsilon} + k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0\right], \qquad
\Gamma_p = k_{\rm spo} + k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0 e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)},
\tag{7}

J_{se} = k_{\rm cat}\left[\rho_p^0 e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)} - \rho_s^0\right] = k_{\rm cat}\rho_p^0\,\frac{e^{-\mu}-1}{e^{\varepsilon}+x},
\tag{8}

where $x = k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0/k_{\rm spo}$. The reactive eigenvalue is

\Gamma \equiv \Gamma_p - \Gamma_s = k_{\rm spo}(1+e^{\varepsilon}) + k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0\left(1+e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)}\right).
\tag{9}

Because $R_e = 0$, one eigenvalue of $\mathcal{J}$ vanishes identically (enzyme conservation); a second vanishes at $q = 0$ (total solute conservation); the third is $-\Gamma$. In the strongly-driven limit ($\varepsilon = -8$, $\mu = 16$), $\Gamma \approx k_{\rm spo} + k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0$. The control parameter is the Damköhler-like ratio

\delta_q \equiv \frac{D_s q^2}{\Gamma}.
\tag{10}

The LEA of Jardat et al. is the regime $\delta_q \to 0$ uniformly in $q$.

2.4 Exact characteristic coefficients

Denoting the entries of $\mathcal{D}$ by $D_{ij}$, the coefficients of Eq. (5) are exact functions of $q^2$:

a_2(q^2) = \left(D_{ee} + D_s + D_p\right)q^2 + \Gamma,
\tag{11}

a_1(q^2) = K_2 q^4 + K_1 q^2,
\tag{12}

with

K_2 = D_{ee}D_s + D_{ee}D_p + D_sD_p - D_s\rho_s^0\mathcal{B}_{es}D_{es} - D_p\rho_p^0\mathcal{B}_{es}D_{es},
\tag{13}

K_1 = \Gamma D_{ee} + D_s\Gamma_p - D_p\Gamma_s.
\tag{14}

The term proportional to $J_{se}(D_{es} - D_{ep})$ in $a_1$ vanishes identically under the equal-excluded-volume assumption $\sigma_{es} = \sigma_{ep}$. The constant term is

a_0(q^2) = q^6\det\mathcal{D} + q^4 C_4,
\tag{15}

with

C_4 = \Gamma_p\left(D_{ee}D_s - D_s\rho_s^0 D_{es}\mathcal{B}_{es}\right) - \Gamma_s\left(D_{ee}D_p - D_p\rho_p^0 D_{es}\mathcal{B}_{es}\right) + J_{se}D_{es}(D_p - D_s).
\tag{16}

2.5 Reduction to Eq. (1) as δ_q → 0 (verification gate)

Expand the long-wavelength branch as $\sigma(q) = \sigma_2 q^2 + \sigma_4 q^4 + \sigma_6 q^6 + O(q^8)$. Matching powers of $q^2$ in Eq. (5):

Order $q^2$: $\Gamma\sigma_2 + C_4 = 0$, hence

\sigma_2 = -\frac{C_4}{\Gamma}.
\tag{17}

Using the steady-state identity for $J_{se}$ and the effective solute diffusivity $D_s^{\rm eff} = (\Gamma_p D_s - \Gamma_s D_p)/\Gamma$, Eq. (16) simplifies to

C_4 = \Gamma\left[D_{ee}D_s^{\rm eff} - D_e\rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{es}\,D_s^{\rm eff}\rho_{sp}^0\mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff}\right],
\tag{18}

so that

\sigma_2 = D_e D_s^{\rm eff}\left(\rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{es}\,\rho_{sp}^0\mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff} - (1 + \rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{ee})\right).
\tag{19}

The condition $\sigma_2 > 0$ is therefore

\rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{es}\,\rho_{sp}^0\mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff} > 1 + \rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{ee}.
\tag{20}

Substituting $\mathcal{B}_{es} = 4\pi\sigma_{es}^3/3$, $\mathcal{B}_{ee} = 4\pi\sigma_{ee}^3/3$, and the explicit $\mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff}$ of Jardat et al., Eq. (20) reduces term-by-term to Eq. (1):

\frac{X\,\sigma_{es}^3}{\sigma_{ee}^3}\,\frac{\alpha-1}{\theta+\alpha(1-\theta)}\,\frac{x(1-e^{-\mu})}{[1+e^{\varepsilon}+x(1+e^{-\varepsilon-\mu})]^2} > \frac{1}{8}+\phi_e.
\tag{21}

This is the verification gate H1: the full linearization reduces to Eq. (21) as $\delta_q \to 0$, coefficient by coefficient.

2.6 Order $q^4$: the finite-rate correction σ₄

Matching at order $q^4$ in Eq. (5):

\sigma_4 = -\frac{\sigma_2^2}{\Gamma} - \frac{\sigma_2}{\Gamma}\left[K_1 + \mathrm{Tr}(\mathcal{D}) - \Gamma\right].
\tag{22}

The critical structural feature is

\sigma_4 = -\frac{\sigma_2^2}{\Gamma} + O(1) \quad \text{as } \Gamma \to \infty.
\tag{23}

When $\Gamma$ is finite, $\sigma_4$ acquires a negative contribution proportional to $\Gamma^{-1}$ from the first term; the second term can become positive when $K_1 + \mathrm{Tr}(\mathcal{D}) - \Gamma > 0$ and $\sigma_2 < 0$. The sign pattern

\sigma_2 < 0, \qquad \sigma_4 > 0, \qquad \sigma_6 < 0
\tag{24}

is the analytic criterion for a Type Iₛ finite-wavelength band at finite $\Gamma$. This pattern is compatible with the two conservation laws: $\sigma(0) = 0$ on all branches, and the band $(\underline{q}, \overline{q})$ with $\underline{q} > 0$ does not violate any conservation constraint.

2.7 Instability classification

Following Cross–Hohenberg, and distinguishing the oscillatory cases carefully:

· Type IIₛ (stationary, long-wavelength): $\sigma_2 > 0$; unstable set includes an interval $(0, q_c)$. This is the LEA result.
· Type Iₛ (stationary, finite-wavelength): $\sigma_2 < 0$, $\sigma_4 > 0$, $\sigma_6 < 0$; unstable set is bounded away from $q = 0$. Conserved-Turing class.
· Type IIₒ (conserved-Hopf, long-wavelength oscillatory): $\operatorname{Im}\sigma \neq 0$ with the real part changing sign at some $q_c \to 0$. This is the generic oscillatory instability for two conservation laws; in coupled conserved systems the Hopf threshold sits at $q_c^2 = 0$.
· Type Iₒ (finite-wavelength oscillatory): $\operatorname{Im}\sigma \neq 0$ at a finite $q_c \neq 0$. This would require a more complex mode coupling and is not expected in the minimal two-conservation-law structure; it must nonetheless be tested and reported if found.

The two oscillatory types must not be conflated: Type IIₒ is the natural candidate, Type Iₒ is a stronger claim.

2.8 Symmetry structure of $J_{se}$ and excluded-volume asymmetry

The exact steady-state evaluation of Eq. (8) shows that $J_{se} = 0$ identically at $\mu = 0$: the reactive cross-coupling vanishes under detailed balance, and no nonreciprocal CIPS is possible at equilibrium. At $\alpha = 1$ with $\sigma_{es} = \sigma_{ep}$, $J_{se}$ does not vanish (unless $\mu = 0$), but the effective nonreciprocal terms in $\mathcal{D}_{\rm LEA}$ become degenerate, stripping the active stress asymmetry required for nonreciprocal phase separation.

A small excluded-volume asymmetry $\sigma_{ep} = \sigma_{es} + \Delta\sigma$ with $|\Delta\sigma| \ll \sigma_{es}$ lifts this degeneracy. Writing $\mathcal{B}_{ep} = \mathcal{B}_{es} + \delta\mathcal{B}$ with $\delta\mathcal{B} = 4\pi\sigma_{es}^2\Delta\sigma + O(\Delta\sigma^2)$:

\delta K_1(\Delta\sigma) = -J_{se}(D_{es} - D_{ep}) = J_{se}D_e\rho_e^0\,\delta\mathcal{B},
\tag{25}

\delta C_4(\Delta\sigma) = \delta\mathcal{B}\,D_e D_p \rho_e^0\rho_p^0\left(\Gamma_s - J_{se}\right).
\tag{26}

Under active drive ($\mu > 0$), $J_{se} < 0$, so $\Delta\sigma > 0$ lowers $\delta K_1$ (destabilizing) while $\Delta\sigma < 0$ raises it. Excluded-volume asymmetry acts as a secondary control parameter that can enlarge the Type Iₛ band at moderate $\alpha$, without requiring extreme diffusivity contrast.

