3. Hypotheses, in priority order

H1 (verification gate)

Statement. The full linearization reduces to Eq. (21) as $\delta_q \to 0$, with coefficients matching term by term and $\Gamma$ given by Eq. (9).

Status in this draft. Established symbolically in §2.5. The reduction from Eq. (19) to Eq. (21) is exact; the identity $J_{se} = k_{\rm cat}\rho_p^0(e^{-\mu}-1)/(e^{\varepsilon}+x)$ and the steady-state ratio used to derive it are exact. H1 is not a result; it is the correctness gate through which every subsequent numerical scan must pass.

H2 (primary): finite rate may generate a Type I instability

Statement. Over the pre-registered parameter box in $(\alpha, X, \Gamma)$, the full dispersion relation (5) either does or does not admit a Type I instability on at least one branch, stationary or oscillatory.

Positive criterion. An unstable interval $(\underline{q}, \overline{q})$ with $\underline{q} > 0$ (Type Iₛ), or an oscillatory unstable mode with $\operatorname{Im}\sigma(q) \neq 0$ and $\operatorname{Re}\sigma(q) > 0$ at the first unstable $q$ (Type IIₒ if $q_c \to 0$; Type Iₒ if $q_c > 0$). The classification report must state which of IIₒ or Iₒ is found.

Negative criterion. Every branch satisfies $\operatorname{Re}\sigma(q) \le 0$ for $q \in (0, \underline{q})$ at every parameter point, i.e. the unstable set always includes an interval $(0, q_c)$.

Inconclusive criterion. The band width $\overline{q} - \underline{q}$ falls below the pre-registered $q$-grid resolution (defined in §5).

Search priority. The physical argument for a Type Iₛ band is a resonance between the reactive and diffusive timescales: the sign pattern (24) requires $\sigma_4$ to overcome $-\sigma_2^2/\Gamma$ from Eq. (22), which is most plausible when $\delta_q = D_s q^2/\Gamma \sim O(1)$ at the candidate $q$. The scan will therefore prioritise the crossover region $\delta_q \approx 1$. This is a strategic priority, not a prediction: no theorem guarantees that $\sigma_4$ becomes positive in this region for any particular $(\alpha, X, \Gamma)$.

Classification report. Even a negative H2 must report (i) the branch structure of all three roots $\sigma_j(q)$, (ii) the stationary/oscillatory character at every instability boundary, (iii) the extent of the parameter box, and (iv) the sign of $(\sigma_2, \sigma_4, \sigma_6)$ at every grid point. A no-go result is publishable only if this classification is complete.

H3 (quantitative): the threshold shift $X_c(\Gamma)$

Statement. The threshold shift is obtained by singular perturbation in $\epsilon = 1/\Gamma$. Writing $X_c(\Gamma) = X_c^{(0)} + \epsilon X_c^{(1)} + O(\epsilon^2)$ and $q_c^2 = \kappa\epsilon$, the marginal condition $C_4 + q^2\det\mathcal{D} = 0$ at $\sigma = 0$ gives

X_c^{(1)} = -\frac{C_4^{(1)}(X_c^{(0)}) + \kappa\det\mathcal{D}}{\left.\partial_X C_4^{(0)}\right|_{X_c^{(0)}}},
\tag{27}

which reproduces the asymptotic structure $X_c(\Gamma) = X_c^{(0)} + \delta_q X_c^{(1)} + O(\delta_q^2)$ with $X_c^{(0)}$ given by Eq. (21).

Caveat (validated C7). The linear ansatz assumes analyticity of $X_c$ in $\delta_q$. In systems with two conservation laws, threshold shifts near codimension-two points can be non-analytic (fractional powers of $\delta_q$). The symbolic analysis in Phase A5 will test this: if the singular perturbation yields a fractional exponent $p \neq 1$, H3 is reported as $X_c(\Gamma) = X_c^{(0)} + c\,\delta_q^p$ with $p$ determined, not as a linear shift.

Kill test. If the shift falls below the resolution of the chosen method, H3 is reported as inconclusive with an upper bound, not as a null claim. Continuum PDEs are the primary tool; Brownian dynamics provide a qualitative check only.

H4 (secondary): the threshold diverges as $\Gamma \to 0$ in the correctly defined limit

Two limits, distinguished. Two limits must be kept separate:

· Equilibrated-ratio limit: $\Gamma \to 0$ with $\rho_s/\rho_p$ held at the steady-state ratio $\theta(\rho_e^0)/(1-\theta(\rho_e^0))$. The system maps onto a binary enzyme–solute mixture with diffusivity $D_s^{\rm eff}$. Benchmark: the depletion threshold $X_{\rm dep}^{\rm bin}(\sigma_{es},\phi_e)$ of the non-reactive binary.
· Frozen-ratio limit: $\Gamma \to 0$ at a fixed, generally nonequilibrium ratio $r \equiv \rho_s/\rho_p$. The system is a passive ternary mixture with frozen composition. Benchmark: the passive ternary depletion threshold $X_{\rm dep}^{\rm ter}(\sigma_{es}, \phi_e, r)$.

Statement. Define $\Delta X(\Gamma) \equiv X_c(\Gamma) - X_{\rm dep}$, with $X_{\rm dep}$ in the appropriate limit. Then $\Delta X(\Gamma) \to 0$ as $\Gamma \to 0$, with a predicted scaling $\Delta X \propto \Gamma^p$ for an exponent $p$ to be determined in Phase A5 (consistent with the H3 caveat).

Explicit caveat (validated R2). The frozen-ratio limit is a formal device, not a biological regime. Any living cell maintains basal turnover; the frozen limit provides an independently computable passive ternary benchmark, and its only function is to detect sign or algebraic errors in the reaction Jacobian and the passive threshold calculation. It must not be interpreted as a physically realizable state.

Kill test. The frozen-reaction threshold must match the independently computed passive depletion threshold $X_{\rm dep}$ at the same densities, excluded volumes, and ratio. A mismatch indicates an error; a match confirms H4.

H5 (conditional on H2): coarsening arrest at $\ell_{\rm rd} \sim \sqrt{D_s/\Gamma}$

Statement. If H2 is positive, coarsening arrests at $\ell_{\rm rd} = c\sqrt{D_s/\Gamma}$ with a prefactor $c$ predicted by the full 3×3 theory. If H2 is negative, coarsening may still arrest nonlinearly; this must be reported as a separate result, and the Raßhofer–Frey sharp-interface theory is the appropriate benchmark.

Two coupling regimes. The Jardat mechanism is intrinsically one in which $\Gamma$ is modulated by the local enzyme density, since the enzyme is itself the phase-separating field. Two regimes must be distinguished:

· Weak-coupling regime (small enzyme-density variations): $\ell_{\rm rd} \approx c\sqrt{D_s/\bar{\Gamma}}$ with $\bar{\Gamma}$ the mean rate. Test this first.
· Strong-coupling regime (large enzyme-density variations): the local screening length varies between enzyme-rich and enzyme-poor domains; domain walls can pin where the local screening length matches the domain size. Report the distribution of domain sizes, not a single $\ell_{\rm rd}$.

Kill test. (i) Full macroscopic coarsening to the box size despite a predicted $\ell_{\rm rd}$ smaller than the box. (ii) Arrest at a length inconsistent with the predicted prefactor. (iii) Saturation time exceeding the simulation time — reported as inconclusive.

