4. Methodology

Phase A — analytic (largely complete in this draft)

A1. Full 3×3 matrix with explicit dependence on $(\rho_e^0, \rho_s^0, \rho_p^0, \mu, \alpha, \sigma_{es})$: done (§2.1–2.3).

A2. Cubic (5) and its exact coefficients: done (§2.4, Eqs. 11–16). The zero modes at $q = 0$ are recovered.

A3. Expansion coefficients $\sigma_2, \sigma_4$: done (§2.5–2.6, Eqs. 17–23). The sign conditions (24) are the analytic Type Iₛ criterion. Remaining: solve the $\sigma_6 < 0$ condition explicitly and map the region in $(\alpha, X, \Gamma, \mu)$ where (24) holds.

A4. Reduction to Eq. (21): done (§2.5).

A5. Singular perturbation for the $\Delta X \propto \Gamma^p$ scaling: partially done (Eq. 27). Remaining: determine whether $p = 1$ (analytic) or fractional; report accordingly.

A6. Symmetry structure of $J_{se}$ and the excluded-volume-asymmetry perturbation: done (§2.8, Eqs. 25–26).

Phase B — continuum numerics

B1. Solve the full PDEs of Eqs. (2)–(3) over the pre-registered box. Map $\sigma(q)$ for all three branches at each point. Prioritise the $\delta_q \approx 1$ region.

B2. Locate every instability boundary. Classify each as Iₛ, IIₛ, Iₒ, or IIₒ — with IIₒ and Iₒ reported separately.

B3. Map $X_c(\Gamma)$ and $X_c(\alpha)$ at fixed drive $\mu$, and compare with Eq. (21) and Eq. (27). Test whether the shift is linear or fractional in $\delta_q$.

B4. Test H4 in both the equilibrated-ratio and frozen-ratio limits, benchmarking against $X_{\rm dep}^{\rm bin}$ and $X_{\rm dep}^{\rm ter}$ respectively.

B5. Compare against the Raßhofer–Frey arrest criterion where the models overlap.

Phase C — Brownian dynamics (conditional on H2)

C1. Rerun the Jardat et al. BD setup, varying only $\tau_{\rm reac}$ (equivalently $\Gamma$), to confirm the sign and order of magnitude of the H3 shift where possible.

C2. Test H5 at three values of $\Gamma$ where H2 is positive.

C3. Report for each point: number of independent runs, Binder-cumulant uncertainty, system size in units of $\ell_{\rm rd}$, and the smallest detectable shift in $X_c$.

C4 (secondary check, validated C8). If Phase B finds no deterministic Type I instability, check whether the structure factor $S(q)$ develops a peak at a finite $q$ in the deterministically stable region, as a secondary diagnostic. This is not a parallel robust outcome; the primary deterministic classification stands regardless. Report as a note, not as a co-equal result.

Phase D — biological mapping

D1. Report $\delta_q = D_s q^2/\Gamma$ at three scales: the enzyme radius, the predicted spinodal wavelength, and the simulation box size.

D2. Explicit unit table for every symbol in $\Gamma$. State whether $k_{\rm cat}$ is a second-order constant (multiplied by $\rho_e$ in molar units) or a first-order turnover number.

D3. Representative parameter values (validated C9), for at least one enzyme–metabolite pair:

Quantity Representative value Note
$D_e$ (enzyme) $10$–$100\ \mu{\rm m}^2/{\rm s}$ globular enzyme in water, 20 °C
$D_s$ (metabolite) $\sim 210\ \mu{\rm m}^2/{\rm s}$ e.g. glutamate, specific conditions
$k_{\rm cat}$ median $\sim 10\ {\rm s}^{-1}$ spans $<1$ to $>10^3\ {\rm s}^{-1}$
$\rho_e$ at $X = 1$ $\sim 3\ {\rm mM}$ for a 5 nm enzyme

Compute $\delta_q$ at the spinodal wavelength for these values and state whether the biological regime sits in the LEA corner or the finite-rate region. Note explicitly that the EPAC/cAMP system is an equilibrium-interaction condensate, not a pure excluded-volume CIPS, and is therefore not a parameter source for this mechanism.

