6. Expected contributions

1. A classification of the full dispersion relation (5) over the pre-registered parameter box, with the LEA result of Eq. (21) identified as the $\delta_q \to 0$ corner and the exact reduction demonstrated symbolically.
2. A definitive statement on the existence of Type I (stationary or oscillatory) instabilities in the enzyme-mediated, excluded-volume CIPS mechanism, positive or negative, with the oscillatory branch reported as IIₒ or Iₒ separately.
3. A finite-rate regime map of CIPS in $(\alpha, X, \Gamma)$, with the LEA boundary, the crossover region $\delta_q \approx 1$, and the Raßhofer–Frey benchmark all marked.
4. The reaction–diffusion length scale $\ell_{\rm rd} = c\sqrt{D_s/\Gamma}$ and its prefactor, conditional on H2; in the strong-coupling regime, the domain-size distribution.
5. A sharp statement of when Eq. (21) applies, expressed as an upper bound on $\delta_q$ at the spinodal wavelength.
6. A quantified biological mapping: the value of $\delta_q$ at the spinodal wavelength for a representative enzyme–metabolite pair, and a statement of whether that value sits in the LEA regime or the finite-rate regime.

