Appendix B: Exact Symbolic Derivation

B.1 Reaction Jacobian and the identity J_{se} = k_{\rm cat}\rho_p^0(e^{-\mu}-1)/(e^{\varepsilon}+x)

At the homogeneous steady state, R_s = 0 gives

0 = k_{\rm spo}[\rho_p^0 - e^{\varepsilon}\rho_s^0] + k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0[\rho_p^0 e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)} - \rho_s^0].
\tag{B1}

Rearranging for \rho_s^0/\rho_p^0:

\frac{\rho_s^0}{\rho_p^0} = \frac{k_{\rm spo} + k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0 e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)}}{k_{\rm spo}e^{\varepsilon} + k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0} = \frac{1 + x e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)}}{e^{\varepsilon}+x},
\tag{B2}

where x = k_{\rm cat}\rho_e^0/k_{\rm spo}. The Jacobian entry is

J_{se} = k_{\rm cat}[\rho_p^0 e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)} - \rho_s^0] = k_{\rm cat}\rho_p^0\left[e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)} - \frac{1+x e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)}}{e^{\varepsilon}+x}\right].
\tag{B3}

Putting over a common denominator,

J_{se} = k_{\rm cat}\rho_p^0\,\frac{e^{\varepsilon}e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)} + x e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)} - 1 - x e^{-(\varepsilon+\mu)}}{e^{\varepsilon}+x} = k_{\rm cat}\rho_p^0\,\frac{e^{-\mu}-1}{e^{\varepsilon}+x}.
\tag{B4}

Consequences. J_{se}=0 identically at \mu=0. Under active drive (\mu>0), e^{-\mu}-1<0, so J_{se}<0 for all x>0. This sign is fixed by the direction of the drive and is not an adjustable parameter.

B.2 Vanishing of the J_{se}(D_{es}-D_{ep}) term in a_1

With \sigma_{es}=\sigma_{ep}, the excluded-volume coefficients satisfy \mathcal{B}_{es}=\mathcal{B}_{ep}, hence D_{es}=D_e\rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{es}=D_{ep}. In the expansion of \det(\sigma\mathbb{I}+q^2\mathcal{D}-\mathcal{J}), the coefficient a_1 collects terms linear in \sigma. The contribution proportional to J_{se} enters through the (s,p) off-diagonal block of \mathcal{D} and is proportional to D_{es}-D_{ep}. Under equal excluded volumes this term vanishes identically:

a_1(q^2) = K_2 q^4 + K_1 q^2, \qquad K_1 = \Gamma D_{ee} + D_s\Gamma_p - D_p\Gamma_s.
\tag{B5}

This is why the symmetric model has a one-parameter family of cancellations that the asymmetric perturbation \sigma_{ep}=\sigma_{es}+\Delta\sigma breaks.

B.3 The coefficient C_4 and the reduction to Eq. (21)

The constant term in q^2 of \det(\sigma\mathbb{I}+q^2\mathcal{D}-\mathcal{J}) is a_0(q^2)=q^6\det\mathcal{D}+q^4 C_4, with

C_4 = \Gamma_p(D_{ee}D_s - D_{es}D_{se}) - \Gamma_s(D_{ee}D_p - D_{ep}D_{pe}) + J_{se}(D_{es}D_p - D_{ep}D_s).
\tag{B6}

Using D_{es}=D_{ep} and the definition D_s^{\rm eff}=(\Gamma_p D_s - \Gamma_s D_p)/\Gamma, Eq. (B6) collapses to

C_4 = \Gamma\left[D_{ee}D_s^{\rm eff} - D_e\rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{es}\,D_s^{\rm eff}\rho_{sp}^0\mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff}\right].
\tag{B7}

The order-q^2 matching \Gamma\sigma_2 + C_4 = 0 then gives

\sigma_2 = D_e D_s^{\rm eff}\left(\rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{es}\,\rho_{sp}^0\mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff} - (1+\rho_e^0\mathcal{B}_{ee})\right).
\tag{B8}

The instability condition \sigma_2>0 is therefore Eq. (20) of the main text. Substituting the definitions of X,\phi_e,\alpha,\theta,x and the explicit form of \mathcal{B}_{es}^{\rm eff} from Jardat et al. reduces Eq. (B8) term by term to Eq. (21). The reduction is not an analogy; it is an algebraic identity.

B.4 Order-q^4 coefficient and the Type Iₛ criterion

Matching at order q^4 in the cubic (5) gives

\sigma_4 = -\frac{\sigma_2^2}{\Gamma} - \frac{\sigma_2}{\Gamma}\left[K_1 + \mathrm{Tr}(\mathcal{D}) - \Gamma\right].
\tag{B9}

The structural feature is that the first term is negative-definite and scales as \Gamma^{-1} at fixed \sigma_2. The second term can change sign. The Type Iₛ sign pattern

\sigma_2 < 0, \qquad \sigma_4 > 0, \qquad \sigma_6 < 0
\tag{B10}

requires the second term in Eq. (B9) to overcome the first when \sigma_2<0. This is the analytic criterion implemented in Phase A3. No claim is made that Eq. (B10) is realized for any point in the pre-registered box; that is the content of H2.

The conservation constraint is satisfied automatically: \sigma(0)=0 on all branches, and the band (\underline{q},\overline{q}) with \underline{q}>0 is compatible with this constraint. The conservation laws forbid a nonzero uniform mode; they do not forbid a bounded band.

B.5 The two oscillatory types

The linear problem has two conservation laws (enzyme number and total solute number). At q=0 the eigenvalues are 0,0,-\Gamma. Oscillatory behaviour can arise only at finite q, through the coupling of the two conserved modes via the nonreciprocal terms in \mathcal{D}. Two cases must be distinguished:

· Type IIₒ (conserved-Hopf): \operatorname{Im}\sigma(q)\neq 0 with the real part changing sign as q\to 0. In coupled conserved systems the Hopf threshold sits at q_c^2=0; this is the generic large-scale oscillatory instability for two conservation laws. The amplitude equation for this bifurcation is the conserved analogue of the complex Ginzburg–Landau equation.
· Type Iₒ (finite-wavelength oscillatory): \operatorname{Im}\sigma\neq 0 at a finite q_c\neq 0. This requires a stronger mode coupling and is not expected in the minimal two-conservation-law structure. It must nonetheless be tested and reported if found.

The classification report in H2 must state which of IIₒ or Iₒ is found. Conflating them would be a classification error.

B.6 Excluded-volume asymmetry as a singular perturbation

With \sigma_{ep}=\sigma_{es}+\Delta\sigma, |\Delta\sigma|\ll\sigma_{es},

\mathcal{B}_{ep} = \mathcal{B}_{es} + 4\pi\sigma_{es}^2\Delta\sigma + O(\Delta\sigma^2) \equiv \mathcal{B}_{es}+\delta\mathcal{B}.
\tag{B11}

The previously cancelling term in a_1 becomes

\delta K_1(\Delta\sigma) = -J_{se}(D_{es}-D_{ep}) = J_{se}D_e\rho_e^0\,\delta\mathcal{B}.
\tag{B12}

Under \mu>0, J_{se}<0, so \delta K_1<0 for \Delta\sigma>0: a larger product excluded volume lowers the linear damping coefficient. The constant-term perturbation is

\delta C_4(\Delta\sigma) = \delta\mathcal{B}\,D_e D_p\rho_e^0\rho_p^0(\Gamma_s-J_{se}).
\tag{B13}

The asymmetry therefore provides a secondary control parameter that can enlarge the Type Iₛ band at moderate \alpha, without requiring extreme diffusivity contrast. It does not create a new instability class; it shifts the boundaries of the existing one.

B.7 The singular-perturbation expansion for X_c(\Gamma)

Write \epsilon=1/\Gamma and expand X_c=X_c^{(0)}+\epsilon X_c^{(1)}+\cdots. At marginality for a stationary mode, C_4+q^2\det\mathcal{D}=0. Expanding C_4 about X_c^{(0)} and using C_4^{(0)}(X_c^{(0)})=0,

\epsilon\left[(\partial_X C_4^{(0)})X_c^{(1)}+C_4^{(1)}+\kappa\det\mathcal{D}\right]+O(\epsilon^2)=0,
\tag{B14}

where q_c^2=\kappa\epsilon. Hence

X_c^{(1)} = -\frac{C_4^{(1)}(X_c^{(0)})+\kappa\det\mathcal{D}}{\left.\partial_X C_4^{(0)}\right|_{X_c^{(0)}}}.
\tag{B15}

This reproduces the asymptotic structure X_c(\Gamma)=X_c^{(0)}+\delta_q X_c^{(1)}+O(\delta_q^2) if and only if the expansion is analytic. The symbolic analysis in Phase A5 must check whether q_c^2 scales linearly with \epsilon; if it scales as \epsilon^p with p\neq 1, the threshold shift is X_c=X_c^{(0)}+c\,\delta_q^p and H3 must be reported with the measured exponent. The linear ansatz is a working hypothesis, not a theorem.

