Appendix C: Numerical Protocols and Operational Definitions

C.1 The q-grid and the “inconclusive” criterion

The pre-registered q-grid is logarithmic: q\in[10^{-3},1] in units of 1/R_e, with 200 points. A positive H2 result requires an unstable interval (\underline{q},\overline{q}) with \underline{q}>0 containing at least five consecutive q-points with \operatorname{Re}\sigma>0. Fewer than five points is classified as inconclusive (“band width below resolution”). This definition is fixed before Phase B begins and is not adjusted after seeing the data.

For the oscillatory branches, the criterion is \operatorname{Im}\sigma(q)\neq 0 at the first q at which \operatorname{Re}\sigma changes sign, with the same five-point requirement on the extent of the unstable interval.

C.2 Continuum PDE solver (Phase B)

The reaction–diffusion system (2)–(3) is stiff in the regime \Gamma\gg D_s q^2. We use an implicit–explicit (IMEX) scheme: diffusion is treated implicitly (spectral or Crank–Nicolson), reaction explicitly. IMEX Runge–Kutta methods are the standard choice for stiff reaction–diffusion systems of this type and have been validated on Turing-pattern and phase-separation benchmarks. The spatial discretization is pseudospectral on a periodic box of side L=2\pi/q_{\min}, with q_{\min}=10^{-3}/R_e. Time-step convergence is checked by halving \Delta t and requiring the growth rate to agree to within 10^{-3} in the linear regime.

For each parameter point, the dispersion relation \sigma(q) is obtained either (i) by direct eigendecomposition of the 3\times3 matrix at each q, or (ii) by fitting the early-time growth of Fourier modes in a weakly perturbed simulation. Method (i) is exact and is the primary tool; method (ii) is a consistency check.

C.3 Brownian dynamics (Phase C)

The BD protocol follows Jardat et al.: overdamped Langevin dynamics for N_e enzymes and N_s+N_p solute particles, with the same pair potentials and reaction rules. The only parameter varied relative to the published setup is \tau_{\rm reac} (equivalently \Gamma), implemented by rescaling k_{\rm spo} and k_{\rm cat} at fixed ratio x.

Domain-size statistics are extracted from the enzyme-density field. Two observables are reported:

· Binder cumulant U_4 = 1 - \langle \rho_e^4\rangle/(3\langle \rho_e^2\rangle^2). In the symmetric two-phase regime U_4\to 2/3; in the disordered phase U_4\to 0. U_4 is used to locate the phase boundary, not to measure the domain size.
· Domain size R(t) from the first moment of the structure factor, R(t)=2\pi/\langle q\rangle_t with \langle q\rangle_t=\sum_q q S(q,t)/\sum_q S(q,t). For a conserved scalar without hydrodynamics, the expected late-time growth is R\sim t^{1/3} (Binder–Stauffer Brownian coalescence). Departure from this power law and saturation at a finite R are the signatures of arrest.

For each state point, N_{\rm run}\ge 10 independent runs are averaged. The uncertainty on R(t) is the standard error across runs. System size is quoted in units of the predicted \ell_{\rm rd} so that finite-size effects can be assessed.

C.4 The secondary noise diagnostic (C4)

If Phase B finds no deterministic Type I instability, the structure factor S(q) in the deterministically stable region is examined for a finite-q peak. This is a secondary diagnostic, not a co-equal robust outcome. A peak is reported only if it is stable across independent runs and survives a doubling of the system size; otherwise it is recorded as a finite-size artefact. The primary conclusion of the paper rests on the deterministic classification (H2) regardless of the outcome of this diagnostic.

