Appendix D: Parameter Derivations for the Biological Mapping

D.1 The two limits of H4, operationally defined

· Equilibrated-ratio limit. \Gamma\to 0 with \rho_s/\rho_p held at the steady-state ratio of Eq. (B2). Numerically, this is implemented by reducing k_{\rm spo} and k_{\rm cat} at fixed x, and re-equilibrating the homogeneous state at each step so that R_s=0 is maintained. The benchmark is X_{\rm dep}^{\rm bin}(\sigma_{es},\phi_e) of the non-reactive binary at the same densities.
· Frozen-ratio limit. \Gamma\to 0 at fixed r=\rho_s/\rho_p, generally away from the steady-state ratio. The benchmark is X_{\rm dep}^{\rm ter}(\sigma_{es},\phi_e,r) of the passive ternary.

The frozen-ratio limit is a formal device. Any living cell maintains basal turnover; the limit is used only to provide an independently computable passive threshold against which the reaction Jacobian can be checked. A mismatch between the computed frozen-limit threshold and X_{\rm dep}^{\rm ter} signals an algebraic error in \mathcal{J} or in the passive calculation.

