Appendix F: Summary of Pre-Registered Definitions

For reproducibility, the operational definitions used in the pre-registration table are collected here.

1. q-resolution. 200 logarithmically spaced points in q\in[10^{-3},1]/R_e. “Below resolution” means fewer than five consecutive points with \operatorname{Re}\sigma>0 at q>0.
2. Stationary vs. oscillatory. A branch is stationary at onset if |\operatorname{Im}\sigma|/\operatorname{Re}\sigma<10^{-2} at the marginal q; otherwise it is oscillatory.
3. IIₒ vs. Iₒ. If the first unstable q satisfies q_c<5\times10^{-3}/R_e (the lower quartile of the grid in log space), the oscillatory instability is classified IIₒ; if q_c\ge5\times10^{-3}/R_e, it is classified Iₒ.
4. Threshold estimate and uncertainty. X_c is located by bisection on the sign of \max_q\operatorname{Re}\sigma(q) with tolerance 10^{-3} in X. The uncertainty quoted is the half-width of the bracket at convergence; “2\sigma” in the pre-registration table refers to twice this half-width.
5. Arrest length. In the weak-coupling regime, \ell_{\rm rd} is the value at which R(t) first satisfies |d\ln R/d\ln t|<0.05 and remains so for at least one decade in t. In the strong-coupling regime, the full distribution P(R) at the final time is reported and no single \ell_{\rm rd} is claimed.
6. H4 scaling exponent. p is obtained by linear regression of \ln|\Delta X| against \ln\Gamma over at least one decade in \Gamma with R^2>0.95; if R^2<0.95, the exponent is reported as undetermined and H4 as inconclusive.

These definitions are fixed before Phase B begins and are not modified in response to the data.

